{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 80,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Nobel prize in physics this year went to black holes. Generally speaking.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5760,
    "dDurationMs": 4483,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Specifically it was shared by the astronomers who revealed  to us the Milky Way’s "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10243,
    "dDurationMs": 5437,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "central black hole and by Roger Penrose, who proved that in general relativity, every black hole contains a place of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15680,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "infinite gravity - a singularity. But the true \nimpact of Penrose’s singularity theorem  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22560,
    "dDurationMs": 6584,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is much deeper - it leads us to the limits Einstein’s  great theory and to the origin of the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34160,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Black holes have haunted our \ntheories of gravity since the 1700s.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38880,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When John Mitchell and Pierre-Simon Laplace explored Newton’s law of universal gravitation,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44160,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they realized the possibility of a \nstar so massive that it would prevent  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47920,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even light from escaping its surface. Few people took these “dark stars” seriously - especially  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 54080,
    "dDurationMs": 6564,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when we learned that light didn’t really behave as Mitchell, Laplace, and even Newton assumed.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60644,
    "dDurationMs": 5146,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And then of course we figured out that not \neven gravity worked as Newton had told us.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65790,
    "dDurationMs": 5090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 1915, Newtonian gravity was superseded by Einstein’s general theory of relativity.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71680,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that wasn’t the end of black holes \nor dark stars; it was their resurrection."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77120,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Karl Schwarzschild solved the equations of general relativity soon after Einstein published them,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82160,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "revealing that a sufficiently dense ball of matter would be surrounded by a surface where time froze.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89440,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Beneath that “event horizon” all matter, light, space itself was doomed to fall inwards towards a central  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96560,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "point. At that so-called singularity, the \ngravitational field becomes infinite. But  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101920,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physicists tend to be dubious about infinities - more often than not they turn out to be a glitch  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107920,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the theory. Einstein himself doubted that  the black holes could form in the real universe,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113200,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and even if they could, they certainly \nshouldn’t harbor singularities."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118000,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "After all, Schwarzschild’s solution \ndidn’t say anything about HOW  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121680,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a clump of matter could reach the densities  high enough to produce an event horizon - nor  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127680,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even whether such dense matter would \nreally contract into a single point.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131440,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It only showed that, once so-contracted, \nthe resulting black hole was stable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136640,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the intriguing and terrifying possibility \nthat black holes might really exist  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 141520,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inspired some of our greatest minds \nto find ways they could exist.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146800,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 1939 Robert Oppenheimer and Hartland  Snyder showed that a perfectly spherical,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152640,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perfectly smooth ball of dust could \ncollapse into a Schwarzschild black hole,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 157760,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "singularity and all. Makes sense - if all \nparticles are falling directly towards each  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162720,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other on a perfect collision course, the \nmath has to land them all in the center."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168800,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But this didn’t convince anyone - what in our universe is perfectly spherical or smooth?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175520,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It was assumed that normal, messy objects could \nnever collapse into a perfect point. Surely any  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181600,
    "dDurationMs": 6269,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tiny deviation from spherical symmetry would cause particles to miss each other at the last instant.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 187869,
    "dDurationMs": 4611,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That might, for example, result in the infalling matter flying back outwards again. So, for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 192480,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "example, a star that collapses at the end of its life might entirely rebound as a giant explosion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 198560,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And now we come to the mid 1960s. Roy Kerr has just figured out how to describe a rotating black  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 203440,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hole in Einstein’s theory. In it, the central point of infinite gravity is spun out into a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207920,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ring - but it was a singularity nonetheless. The Kerr solution was still highly symmetrical,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213120,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so the same old arguments held against \nthese things forming in reality."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218400,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It took a young Camridge physicist named Roger Penrose to prove that black hole singularities  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 223280,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "were utterly unavoidable in Einstein’s theory. Penrose’s singularity paper is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228800,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "deceptively short - just a couple of pages in Physical Review Letters  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 233360,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However that brevity is deceptive. But some say this 1965 paper produced the first truly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240320,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "new advances in general relativity, half \na century after the theory was published."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246400,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what did Penrose discover? How did he manage to peer into the mathematical heart of the black  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 251200,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hole? And why did this deserve a Nobel prize? Penrose set out to show that an event horizon  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 257360,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and a singularity would form for any distribution of matter, no matter how messy, as long as that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 263040,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter was compacted into a small enough volume of space. At least according to general relativity.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 269680,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And his singularity theorem succeeded in that - but it succeeded in so much more, as we’ll see."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 275840,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today I’m going to give you a sense \nof Penrose’s breakthrough, and why it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 279360,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "revolutionized general relativity. For a more complete picture I can refer you to a good grad  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 284560,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "program in physics - but let’s see how much we can do in a handful of minutes. Basically,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290400,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Penrose showed that according to Einstein’s theory plus a couple of assumptions,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294560,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black holes must contain singularities. And this is true regardless of how the black hole formed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301280,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He did it in a clever way - by showing that the grid of spacetime literally comes to an end inside  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307600,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a black hole. In general relativity we map the fabric of spacetime with gridlines that we call  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 313600,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "geodesics. These are the paths traveled by an object in free fall in a gravitational field.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 319200,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The path traveled by a ray of light is \ncalled a null geodesic. They are the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 324560,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gold-standard for gridding up spacetime. \nNull geodesics traveling into or past any  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 330640,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravitational field tend to be drawn \ntogether - to converge, or be focused.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 335680,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is gravitational lensing. Gravitational \nfields produced by regular matter  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 340640,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "always produce this convergence. It would take negative mass or negative pressure  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345360,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to cause light rays to diverge. This focusing property of gravitational fields is called the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350320,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "weak energy condition of general relativity, and it almost certainly holds in black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 355360,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So null geodesics traveling down into a \nblack hole are going to converge - no big  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 360000,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "surprise there. But the crazy thing about \nblack holes is that null geodesics beneath  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 364000,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the event horizon that are trying \nto travel outwards also converge.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 368160,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Actually, the whole idea of the event horizon is tricky here - there are multiple ways to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 373520,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "define an event horizon depending on where you sit compared to the black hole. Penrose came up with a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 379600,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more precise idea - that of the trapped surface. That’s any closed surface that has this property  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 386400,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that null geodesics - and so any light - pointed outwards from the surface actually move downwards.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392240,
    "dDurationMs": 6059,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Any closed surface “inside” the black \nhole is considered a trapped surface."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 398299,
    "dDurationMs": 4741,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Penrose showed that at least some of \nthe parallel null geodesics leaving any  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 403040,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "trapped surface, either up or down, had \nto converge. Had to cross each other and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 408240,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "come to a focus. And he also showed that, for rays departing a trapped surface,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 414560,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s meaningless to continue to track \nthe progression of space and time  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 419040,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "beyond one of these focal points. In other \nwords, space and time end at the focus."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425760,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I can only give you a vague sense of why this is the case. Penrose proves it by showing that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 431280,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "impossible contradictions arise otherwise. Imagine a pair of light rays emerging from the same point  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 437920,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and then focused back towards each other. Both are equally the shortest path between those points.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 444080,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now imagine extending one of those paths just a little. The distance from the starting point  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448960,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the end of that extension is still the \nsame whether we take the left or right arc.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454080,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But now we can find an even shorter path \n- one obtained by smoothing out this kink.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 459520,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that has to be shorter \nthan both original paths.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 463120,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So there’s the contradiction - we found a shorter path than the supposedly shortest paths. This  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 467760,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tells us that the null geodesic does not continue past the focal points AS a null geodesic. Null  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 474800,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "geodesics terminate at these focal points within a black hole. We call this geodesic incompleteness.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 481120,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because they are the grid we use to map space and time, geodesic incompleteness means space  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 485440,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and/or time end at these termination points. It doesn’t just freeze, they literally cease."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 491840,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s try an analogy - the geodesics we \nuse to map the surface of the earth are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 495680,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "longitude and latitude. Lines of longitude \ncome to a focus at the north and south poles.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 500800,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A line of longitude is the shortest distance \nto the north pole - the quickest way for you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 506000,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to increase your “northness”. But if you \ntravel past the north pole you’re no longer  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 510480,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the shortest path to any new point that you reach, and at the same time you reached the end  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 516240,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of north - maxed your northness - and started traveling south again. Well, in a black hole you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 521840,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "don’t reach the end of north, you reach the end of time or space. They are dead-ends to reality."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529600,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So yeah, geodesic incompleteness is pretty freaky. Prior to Penrose it was generally  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534480,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "held that geodesics could be traced \nindefinitely into the past and future.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 538640,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All of spacetime should be a smooth, if \ncurved structure - a manifold - cleanly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 543600,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "defined everywhere. Penrose showed that \nspace and time could have holes in it.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 549200,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These holes tend to be associated with infinite spacetime curvature - infinite gravity. In other  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 554320,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "words, singularities. Penrose didn’t actually show what type of singularity would form in a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 559200,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole - just that some type was inevitable. In a Schwarzschild black hole, time ceases at the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565280,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "point-like central singularity, while in Kerr \nblack holes space ends at the ring singularity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571440,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With Roger Penrose’s discovery, black holes and the singularities within had to be taken more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 575920,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "seriously. But the true utility of his singularity theorem went well beyond black holes. Just after  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 582160,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Penrose published his paper, a young graduate student was inspired to apply the singularity  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 587200,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theorem in a very different way. That student was Stephen Hawking. In his PhD thesis Hawking  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593680,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "showed that you could use the same arguments to investigate the behavior of geodesics traced  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 598400,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "backwards through our entire universe towards the Big Bang. Now at this point we’d known for 40  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 603520,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "years that the universe is expanding, and probably started in a much denser state. In fact the latter  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 609760,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "had been verified by the detection of the cosmic microwave background just the prior year in 1964."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 615760,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We knew that geodesics converge towards each other looking backwards in time, but doesn’t mean they  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 621920,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "had to all meet. They could, for example, miss and weave past each other in an insanely dense but not  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 627920,
    "dDurationMs": 8299,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "infinitely dense knot. This might be the case if the universe underwent cyclic big bounces."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 636219,
    "dDurationMs": 3941,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hawking showed that Penrose’s arguments  about black holes also applied to the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640160,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe - that geodesics traced backwards in an expanding universe had to truly meet - to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646000,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "form a singularity, which meant they had to terminate. Time itself could not be traced  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 651920,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "beyond this point - which suggested that time really started at the Big Bang. Hawking and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 657280,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Penrose further developed these ideas together, publishing a clean proof in 1970. We now call the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 664480,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "combined proofs of geodesic incompleteness the Penrose-Hawking Singularity Theorems."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 670960,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is probably the right time to tell you how the singularity theorems must be wrong - or at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 675040,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "least point to wrongness. They give us the \npredictions of pure general relativity,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 679680,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and assume the various energy conditions. We know that general relativity is not the entire  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 686160,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "picture - and this might be the most powerful result of the singularity theorems. Remember,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 692320,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we should be dubious when we see infinities and singularities in our theories. Penrose  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 697440,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "showed us that singularities are unavoidable in general relativity, and so GR must break down at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 704000,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "those points. The resolution must be the union of general relativity and quantum mechanics - a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 709200,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theory of quantum gravity, from which both quantum mechanics and relativity are just approximations.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 714800,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Such a theory may tell us what really happens to geodesics when they approach and merge. And even,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 720560,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hopefully, what geodesics - and space and time - really are. Penrose’s singularity  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 725440,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theorem is a big part of what set us on the path to this yet-undiscovered greater theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 731120,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Roger Penrose won this year’s Nobel prize  in physics for his contributions to our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 735200,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "theoretical understanding of black holes. He shared it with Andrea Ghez and Reinhard Genzel,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740560,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "who proved to us the existence of the Milky Way’s central supermassive black hole by monitoring the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 745680,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "crazy orbits of stars in the galactic core. The work of Ghez and Genzel and other astronomers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 751280,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have guaranteed the existence of black holes, which means their are places in the universe where  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 756800,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "general relativity must break down or produce singularities. Nobel-worthy insights, and all from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 763280,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some bright ideas about how light rays travel and terminate at the singular dead ends of spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 771200,
    "dDurationMs": 9600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This week we want to extend our heartfelt thanks to Brody Rao, while we're singularly grateful for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 780800,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your support, our appreciation is by no means geodesically incomplete - rather it extends  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 785600,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "infinitely into the future AND the past. With your help Space Time might do the same. Well, not into  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 791840,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the past - hard to make new past episodes before the first one. Although if anyone could Gabe  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 796560,
    "dDurationMs": 8996,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could - hmmm. Well, for now we’ll keep pushing for the future, thanks to the support of all of you for your support to do that. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 805556,
    "dDurationMs": 3724,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Last week we continued our discussion of \ndeterminism in relativity and the block universe,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 809280,
    "dDurationMs": 4109,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but this time weaving in what quantum \nmechanics had to say about the whole mess.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 813389,
    "dDurationMs": 5442,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Quantum mechanics had a few things to say, but you guys had more. Let’s get to the comments."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 818831,
    "dDurationMs": 7889,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Zoltan asks if there’s any possibility to observe or interact with any of the other world, and if no  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 826720,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how is the idea falsifiable? Actually yeah, we can interact with the other worlds. - AKA branches of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 832240,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the wavefunction. That’s exactly what a quantum interference experiment is seeing - multiple  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 837200,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "possible “realities” overlapping and either \nstacking or canceling out to either strengthen  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 843440,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or cancel out certain ongoing branches of the wavefunction. Many Worlds says we find ourselves  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 849120,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in one of those branches. The problem isn’t that we can’t observe the other branches at all - just  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 854720,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that we can’t observe them on a macroscopic scale. There is one proposal by Steven Weinberg called  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 860080,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Everett-Wheeler telephone that allows a type of communication between worlds, but only works if  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 866000,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum mechanics works in a particular non-linear way We should probably do an episode on it.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 873200,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As for falsifiability - I’ll try to give Sean \nCarroll’s argument for this one. Many Worlds can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 878000,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be considered the “cleanest” interpretation  of quantum mechanics. It’s what you get  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 882880,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when you assume nothing but the evolution of the wavefunction under the Schrodinger equation. Other  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 887680,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interpretations - Copenhagen, pilot wave theory, etc. actually add things to the pure evolution  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 893520,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the wavefunction under the Schrodinger equation. So we can look for those extra  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 898240,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "things - the moment of wavefunction collapse, the guiding function or the empty wavefunctions of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 904000,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pilot wave theory - if we continue to try and fail to detect these required additions, then  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 910160,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“pure quantum mechanics” becomes more likely, and some would argue that means many worlds. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 916000,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Michal Grno points out that we forgot to mention Carlo Rovelli's relational QM interpretation.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 922160,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Actually, I didn’t forget - I had a whole section on it but realized there was no way to do it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 926160,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "justice especially because we haven’t done an episode on that before. So how about we just do  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 931520,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that episode. Because I agree - relational quantum \nmechanics is a really profound perspective,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 936240,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in which the “real” quantum states aren’t the entities but the relationships between the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 941040,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "entities - pretty relevant for addressing the \nreality of other parts of the block universe.\n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 946160,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Alan Foxman struggles with the concept that there’s no objective “now”. Even though we can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 952080,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only see the universe of the past - e.g. a star 100 light years away being a century in the past.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 957680,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Surely if we could teleport there we’d see the modern version. That’s true, but here’s the issue:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 962720,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if you and your friend do the same teleport, you’ll end up at different times depending  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 969920,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on your relative velocities. Your velocities \ndetermine your definition of the present. \n "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 975360,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is all very hard and Coffea gives \nus a way out. This could be “That moment  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 981440,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when a boltzmann brain is the simplest \nexplanation.” You’re just random particles  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 986640,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "accidentally assembled from the void with your current memories and experience of the world  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 990880,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and are about to evaporate back into it. Prove I’m wrong! There, science, done! Maybe I’ll  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 997040,
    "dDurationMs": 5868,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "teach next semester this way and walk out 15 seconds into the first lecture. Nice."
    } ]
  } ]
}
